{"id":"min-cost-to-connect-all-points","title":"Min Cost to Connect All Points","difficulty":"Medium","pattern":"Advanced Graphs","tags":["Graph","Minimum Spanning Tree","Union Find","Heap / Priority Queue"],"companies":{"google":2,"amazon":2,"microsoft":1},"description":"You are given `points`, a list of distinct integer coordinates `[x, y]` on a plane. Connecting two points costs their **Manhattan distance** `|x1 - x2| + |y1 - y2|`.\n\nReturn the minimum total cost to connect all points so that there is exactly one simple path between every pair of points (i.e. a spanning tree).","examples":[{"input":"points = [[0,0],[2,2],[3,10],[5,2],[7,0]]","output":"20","explanation":"Edges (0,0)-(2,2)=4, (2,2)-(5,2)=3, (5,2)-(7,0)=4, (2,2)-(3,10)=9 give 20, the cheapest spanning tree."},{"input":"points = [[3,12],[-2,5],[-4,1]]","output":"18"},{"input":"points = [[0,0]]","output":"0"}],"constraints":["1 <= points.length <= 1000","-10^6 <= xi, yi <= 10^6","All points are distinct."],"hints":["Every pair of points is a potential edge, so this is a minimum spanning tree on a complete graph with n^2 edges.","Kruskal works (sort all n^2 edges, union-find), but Prim's algorithm without a heap is O(n^2) time and O(n) space, which is better on a dense graph.","Prim: keep `best[v]` = cheapest edge from the tree to v. Each round pick the unvisited v with the smallest best, add it, then relax every other unvisited point against v."],"function":"minCostConnectPoints","signature":"def minCostConnectPoints(self, points: List[List[int]]) -> int:","arg_types":{"points":"List[List[int]]"},"return_type":"int","compare":"exact","tests":[{"input":{"points":[[0,0],[2,2],[3,10],[5,2],[7,0]]},"output":20},{"input":{"points":[[3,12],[-2,5],[-4,1]]},"output":18},{"input":{"points":[[0,0]]},"output":0},{"input":{"points":[[0,0],[1,1],[1,0],[-1,1]]},"output":4},{"input":{"points":[[-1000000,-1000000],[1000000,1000000]]},"output":4000000},{"input":{"points":[[0,0],[919,1838],[838,1676],[757,1514],[676,1